FRONTEND + AUTHENTICATION MISSION — ADD A REAL USER LOGIN SYSTEM

Application: DROX Trading Post (`trading_post`). Priority: Critical / Blocking.

Add a complete, elite-production-grade user authentication system to `trading_post`.

This is NOT a visual mockup.

The login must actually authenticate users, establish an authenticated session, protect the dashboard, and integrate cleanly with the existing backend and frontend.

============================================================
PRESERVE THE EXISTING DESIGN
============================================================

DO NOT redesign the existing interface.

Keep the current:

- cyberpunk / neon-carbon aesthetic
- HUD styling
- animations
- particles
- typography
- glow effects
- transitions
- terminal aesthetic
- visual hierarchy
- specific existing fonts: Orbitron, JetBrains Mono, Rajdhani, Chakra Petch, Exo 2
- scanlines
- tech-brackets
- HUD header and sidebar
- the existing `.login-overlay` and `.login-card` structure already present in `index.html`

The login screen should feel like it belongs to the existing application.

Do not turn it into a generic corporate login page.

Build the authentication UI using the existing design language.

Do not create a second login page or redesign the card.

============================================================
SECURITY FIRST
============================================================

Inspect the existing backend architecture before implementing authentication.

Do not invent an authentication architecture without checking the repository first.

Implement proper authentication rather than frontend-only protection.

The browser must NEVER be trusted to determine whether a user is authenticated.

The backend must enforce authentication on protected resources.

============================================================
CURRENT ARCHITECTURE — READ FIRST
============================================================

Before writing any code, inspect these existing files thoroughly:

- `server.py` / api — existing FastAPI routes and the current (stub) WebSocket
- `auth.py` — already contains `hash_password`, `verify_password`, `new_session_id`, `session_expiry`
- `repository.py` — already has user + session methods
- `migrations.py` — already defines `users` and `sessions` tables
- `config.py` — `session_ttl_minutes`, `session_secret`, bootstrap settings
- `runtime.py` / Orchestrator — background loops that must respect auth state
- `index.html` — existing login overlay + HUD that must be reused, not replaced

The application already has partial auth scaffolding. Complete and harden it. Do not start from zero.

Respect the current Repository, Settings, Orchestrator, domain models, and error hierarchy. Extend — do not rewrite.

============================================================
USER ACCOUNT MODEL
============================================================

Create a proper user/account model backed by PostgreSQL.

Evaluate the existing schema before creating anything.

At minimum support:

- unique user ID
- username or email
- password hash
- account status
- created timestamp
- updated timestamp
- last login timestamp
- role/authorization level
- authentication/session state as appropriate
- failed login count
- locked-until timestamp (nullable, for temporary lockout)

Roles are, at minimum: viewer | operator | admin.

Do NOT store plaintext passwords.

Use a modern password hashing algorithm such as Argon2id or an equally appropriate password-hashing implementation already supported by the project.

The existing implementation in `auth.py` already uses PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256. Upgrade to Argon2id only if it is already available as a dependency; otherwise harden and reuse the existing implementation rather than introducing a competing one.

Never log passwords.

Never return password hashes through an API.

Never return session tokens in a login response body either — a session token belongs only in the HttpOnly cookie, never in a JSON payload.

============================================================
SESSION AUTHENTICATION
============================================================

Use secure server-side authentication/session semantics appropriate to this application.

Prefer secure HTTP-only cookies for browser authentication rather than exposing long-lived authentication tokens to JavaScript.

Cookies should use appropriate:

- HttpOnly
- Secure
- SameSite
- expiration/max-age

settings based on the actual deployment environment.

Sessions should be:

- server-generated using a cryptographically random session ID
- stored server-side only
- bound to user, issued_at, expires_at, IP address, and user-agent
- revocable

Implement session expiration.

Implement logout/session invalidation.

Do not store authentication secrets in localStorage unless there is a compelling, explicitly justified reason.

============================================================
AUTHENTICATION ENDPOINTS
============================================================

Implement real backend endpoints for:

- login
- logout
- current authenticated user/session
- session validation

Concrete route suggestions (adapt to existing routing conventions):

- POST /api/v1/auth/login — no auth required; validates credentials, creates session, sets cookie
- POST /api/v1/auth/logout — auth required; revokes session, clears cookie
- GET /api/v1/me — auth required; returns current user identity (username + role only)

Evaluate whether registration should be enabled.

DO NOT automatically expose public registration if this is intended to be an operator-controlled trading system.

If registration is not appropriate, implement an administrator/bootstrap mechanism instead.

Do not create an unauthenticated account-creation endpoint that allows anyone to create trading accounts.

============================================================
AUTHORIZATION
============================================================

Authentication and authorization are separate concerns.

Implement roles/permissions appropriate to the actual application.

At minimum evaluate:

- viewer
- operator
- administrator

Trading controls must not automatically be available to every authenticated user.

Protect high-impact operations such as:

- emergency kill switch
- autonomous trading start/stop
- manual order submission
- configuration changes
- account administration
- exchange configuration

Use backend authorization checks.

Do not rely on hidden buttons as security.

Authorization matrix (minimum baseline):

Capability                              | viewer | operator | admin
View dashboard / portfolio / signals    |   yes  |   yes    |  yes
Subscribe / unsubscribe market feeds    |   no   |   yes    |  yes
Start / Stop Autonomous                 |   no   |   yes    |  yes
Emergency Kill Switch                   |   no   |   yes    |  yes
Manual order / analyze                  |   no   |   yes    |  yes
User management / config                |   no   |   no     |  yes

All authorization checks must occur on the backend. Frontend hiding of buttons/controls is not security.

============================================================
DASHBOARD PROTECTION
============================================================

The entire trading dashboard must require authentication.

Unauthenticated users should receive the login interface.

Authenticated users should receive the dashboard.

Authentication state should be checked when the page loads.

If the session expires while the dashboard is open:

- detect the unauthorized response
- stop sensitive operations
- disconnect/restrict live data as appropriate
- display a clear session-expired state
- return the user to login

Do not leave an apparently live trading dashboard running after authentication has expired.

============================================================
LOGIN EXPERIENCE
============================================================

Create a login screen that matches the existing visual design.

Include:

- username/email field
- password field
- show/hide password control
- login button
- loading state
- authentication error state
- session state

Do NOT reveal whether a specific username/email exists.

Use generic authentication failure messaging.

Example:

"Invalid credentials."

rather than:

"That username exists but the password is wrong."

Do not leak authentication information.

Reuse the existing `.login-overlay` and `.login-card` structure already present in `index.html`. Do not create a second login page or redesign the card.

============================================================
LOGIN ANIMATION
============================================================

Use the existing animation language.

The transition from:

LOGIN
→
AUTHENTICATING
→
AUTHENTICATED
→
TRADING COCKPIT

should feel intentional and fit the existing HUD aesthetic.

Do not introduce unrelated visual effects.

============================================================
USER IDENTITY IN DASHBOARD
============================================================

Once authenticated, show a subtle operator identity indicator in the existing HUD.

For example:

OPERATOR
username

or:

SESSION
ACTIVE

Do not expose sensitive account information.

Provide a logout control.

============================================================
WEBSOCKET AUTHENTICATION
============================================================

Audit the existing WebSocket implementation.

A user must not be able to bypass HTTP authentication simply by connecting directly to the WebSocket endpoint.

Ensure WebSocket connections are authenticated.

If cookie-based authentication is used, validate the authenticated session during WebSocket establishment.

The control WebSocket endpoint (e.g. `/ws/control`) must validate the session cookie (or equivalent) on connection.

Unauthorized WebSocket connections must be rejected.

Reject unauthenticated connections with close code 4001 or 1008.

If the authenticated session expires, handle the WebSocket appropriately — close the socket cleanly and do not accept further commands from it.

============================================================
API AUTHENTICATION
============================================================

Audit every existing API endpoint.

Classify endpoints as:

PUBLIC
AUTHENTICATED
OPERATOR
ADMIN

API surface classification (example mapping — adapt to actual routes):

Category      | Examples                                                    | Required Auth Level
Public        | /health, /metrics (if enabled)                              | None
Authenticated | /api/v1/me, portfolio, orders, events, AI decisions (read)  | Any valid session
Operator      | Kill, Start/Stop Auto, Subscribe, Analyze                   | operator or admin
Admin         | User management, config changes                             | admin only

Every existing endpoint must be audited and correctly classified. No sensitive data may remain unprotected.

Do not simply put authentication on the dashboard route and leave trading endpoints exposed.

Protect:

- account data
- balances
- positions
- orders
- trades
- PnL
- risk state
- system controls
- trading controls
- configuration
- audit information

Health/readiness endpoints may remain appropriately public if required for deployment, but do not expose sensitive internal information through them.

============================================================
CSRF
============================================================

If authentication uses cookies, implement appropriate CSRF protection for state-changing browser requests.

Protect operations such as:

- order submission
- kill switch
- start/stop trading
- configuration changes
- logout where appropriate

Do not assume SameSite alone is sufficient for every deployment architecture.

============================================================
BRUTE-FORCE PROTECTION
============================================================

Protect login against brute-force attempts.

Implement appropriate:

- rate limiting
- failed-login throttling
- temporary lockout/backoff where appropriate

Reuse the existing `login_max_attempts_per_ip` and `login_lockout_minutes` settings where present rather than introducing parallel configuration.

Do not create an easy denial-of-service vector by permanently locking accounts after trivial failures.

Never permanently lock an account without administrator intervention.

Use IP/user-based controls carefully.

Do not log passwords.

============================================================
SECURITY HEADERS
============================================================

Audit the application's security headers.

Evaluate:

- Content-Security-Policy
- X-Content-Type-Options
- Referrer-Policy
- frame protection
- permissions policy
- secure cookie configuration

Do not blindly add a CSP that breaks the existing dashboard's scripts/styles.

Do not apply a CSP that breaks Chart.js, Google Fonts, or the existing inline scripts unless the HTML is updated accordingly to remain compatible.

Make it compatible with the actual application.

============================================================
PASSWORD MANAGEMENT
============================================================

Implement secure password policy appropriate to the application.

Do not impose arbitrary complexity requirements that encourage weak password reuse.

Prefer a reasonable minimum length and secure password hashing.

If password changes are implemented, require the authenticated user to prove knowledge of the current password before changing it unless an administrator reset flow is being used.

============================================================
ADMIN / BOOTSTRAP
============================================================

Determine how the first administrator account should be created.

Do NOT hard-code credentials.

Do NOT put default production credentials in source code.

A secure bootstrap mechanism may use an explicitly configured environment variable or a one-time initialization command.

Prefer a `DROX_ADMIN_PASSWORD`-style environment variable for first-run bootstrap if an equivalent pattern already exists in the Orchestrator.

If no admin exists and no bootstrap environment variable is set, generate a one-time random password and log it once at WARNING level, following the existing implemented pattern.

Document the bootstrap process.

============================================================
AUDIT LOGGING
============================================================

Authentication events must integrate with the application's audit system.

Record appropriate events such as:

- login success
- login failure
- logout
- session expiration
- account creation
- password change
- authorization failure
- administrative action

Where the codebase already uses snake_case event identifiers, align with the existing convention, e.g.:

- login_success
- login_failure
- logout
- session_expired
- authorization_denied
- kill_switch_requested (already partially present — extend, do not duplicate)

Do not put passwords, session tokens, or sensitive credentials into audit records.

Include trace/correlation IDs where the application supports them.

============================================================
FRONTEND AUTH STATE
============================================================

Create a single authoritative frontend authentication state.

States should include:

CHECKING_SESSION
AUTHENTICATED
UNAUTHENTICATED
AUTHENTICATING
SESSION_EXPIRED
AUTH_ERROR

Do not scatter authentication flags throughout the page.

The dashboard should render based on authenticated state.

============================================================
PROTECT LIVE DATA
============================================================

Do not establish live trading WebSocket connections until authentication has been established.

Do not start sensitive polling before authentication.

Do not fetch:

- balances
- positions
- orders
- trades
- PnL
- account information

for unauthenticated users.

============================================================
LOGOUT
============================================================

Logout must be real.

On logout:

1. Invalidate the server-side session.
2. Close WebSocket connections.
3. Stop live-data subscriptions.
4. Clear client authentication state.
5. Clear sensitive client-side state.
6. Return to the login screen.

Do not merely hide the dashboard.

============================================================
SESSION EXPIRATION
============================================================

Handle expiration gracefully.

If an API request returns unauthorized:

- update authentication state
- stop sensitive operations
- disconnect live trading data
- display a session-expired message
- redirect/show login

Avoid redirect loops.

============================================================
ERROR HANDLING
============================================================

Authentication failures must not crash the application.

Handle:

- invalid credentials
- server unavailable
- database unavailable
- expired session
- malformed responses
- rate limiting
- authorization failures
- network interruption

Give the operator useful but non-sensitive feedback.

============================================================
VISUAL INTEGRATION
============================================================

The login page must look like it was always part of the existing application.

Use the existing:

- colors
- fonts
- borders
- glow
- panels
- animations
- transitions
- HUD elements
- specific existing fonts: Orbitron, JetBrains Mono, Rajdhani, Chakra Petch, Exo 2
- scanlines
- particle system
- tech-brackets
- HUD header and sidebar

Do not introduce a completely separate design system.

The application should feel like:

LOGIN TERMINAL
        ↓
AUTHENTICATION
        ↓
TRADING POST COCKPIT

============================================================
MODEL / PROVIDER PRIVACY
============================================================

Authentication work must NOT reintroduce or expose internal AI implementation details.

Do not display:

- AI model names
- Ollama model names
- cloud model names
- provider names
- prompt information
- internal AI routing

The authenticated operator only needs capability/status information.

============================================================
DATABASE
============================================================

Use the canonical PostgreSQL database.

Use the existing migration system (`migrations.py`). Do not invent a parallel schema.

Add proper migrations for:

- users
- sessions/authentication state
- roles/permissions
- authentication audit events

Do not modify the database manually outside the application's migration system.

Use appropriate indexes and constraints.

============================================================
TESTING
============================================================

Add tests for:

- valid login
- invalid login
- logout
- expired session
- unauthorized API access
- authorized API access
- unauthorized WebSocket access
- authorized WebSocket access
- role-based authorization
- password hashing
- password verification
- brute-force/rate-limit behavior
- CSRF protection
- session invalidation
- administrative controls

Verify that authentication cannot be bypassed by calling backend endpoints directly.

============================================================
FINAL SECURITY AUDIT
============================================================

After implementation, search the entire repository for:

- plaintext passwords
- hard-coded credentials
- default passwords
- exposed session tokens
- secrets in JavaScript
- secrets in HTML
- authentication bypasses
- unprotected trading endpoints
- unprotected WebSockets
- client-only authorization
- model/provider names exposed in UI

Fix every relevant issue found.

============================================================
RECOMMENDED IMPLEMENTATION ORDER
============================================================

1. Audit existing users/sessions tables and `auth.py` / `repository.py` methods.
2. Harden and complete the login / logout / me endpoints.
3. Enforce authentication middleware/dependency on all protected routes.
4. Protect the WebSocket handshake.
5. Wire the frontend auth state machine to the real endpoints (reuse the existing overlay).
6. Add role checks on high-impact commands.
7. Add brute-force protection and audit events.
8. Write tests.
9. Final security sweep (search for secrets, bypasses, exposed tokens).

============================================================
OUT OF SCOPE — DO NOT IMPLEMENT
============================================================

- Public self-registration
- OAuth / social login
- Multi-factor authentication (unless already partially present)
- Password reset via email
- Redesign of any visual component
- Exposure of AI model names or internal routing
- Storing sessions in localStorage or exposing tokens to JavaScript

============================================================
FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
============================================================

The feature is complete only when:

- A user can actually log in.
- Credentials are securely verified.
- Sessions are securely established.
- The dashboard is inaccessible without authentication.
- APIs enforce authentication server-side.
- WebSockets enforce authentication.
- Authorization is enforced server-side.
- Trading controls require appropriate authorization.
- Logout actually invalidates the session.
- Session expiration is handled correctly.
- Login attempts are protected against abuse.
- Authentication events are auditable.
- PostgreSQL stores the authoritative account/session data.
- Database migrations exist.
- No passwords or tokens are exposed.
- No AI model/provider names are exposed.
- The existing dashboard design remains intact.
- The existing animations remain intact.
- The login experience visually belongs to the existing application.
- Existing live-data functionality continues to work after authentication.
- Tests cover both success and failure paths.
- Direct API/WebSocket access cannot bypass authentication.

Do not create a fake login screen.

Build the actual authentication system end-to-end.

Start by inspecting the existing backend, database architecture, API routes, WebSocket implementation, and `index.html`.

Do not deliver a partial or "looks like it works" solution.

Do not ignore the existing design system.

Do not invent a parallel authentication architecture when the scaffolding already exists.
